/E (Nowadays my focus is shifting towards research on cryptosystems that may someday replace Schnorr and ECDSA, without compromising too much on signature size and cryptographic flexibility. i was disappointed enough by lattices that I didn't even bother writing an article on them. But isogenies feel very different. There is structure, rules which can be manipulated. I think more people should be learning about isogenies as a long-term replacement for Schnorr.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [16]
>>
